     Coroutines chapter covers the same mechanism with compiled examples. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A logging library tags every line with the current user ID, stored in a <code>ThreadLocal</code>. After a migration to coroutines, about a third of the log lines lose their tag, always after a network call. Nothing cleared the value. The code simply continued on a different thread, one that had never been given a user ID.</p>

A `suspend` function looks sequential, but it does not hold a thread while it waits. The compiler turns it into a **state machine**: a label for where to continue, and fields for the local variables that must survive the wait. When the awaited result arrives, the machine resumes, possibly on another thread from the dispatcher. This chapter shows that transformation, and the bugs that come from forgetting it.

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: animation -->
### Run <code>fetchAndShowUser()</code> through its state machine

