<!-- @diagram 8a0f8b7b256c3ca5 -->
### Labels, suspensions and threads

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Label 0: Start</strong><small>run by Thread 1</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Return <code>COROUTINE_SUSPENDED</code></strong><small>Thread 1 is freed</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Resume with User</strong><small>a network callback calls <code>resume()</code>; Thread 2 runs it</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Label 1: print user</strong></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Return <code>COROUTINE_SUSPENDED</code></strong><small>Thread 2 is freed</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Resume with avatar → Label 2: Done</strong></div></div><figcaption>Each suspension point splits the function; each resume enters it again at the next label.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Misconceptions :: check -->
**Blocked or suspended?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary><code>Thread.sleep(2000)</code> inside <code>launch</code> on the main dispatcher.</summary>

Blocked. `sleep` holds the main thread, so nothing else runs there: the UI freezes. `delay(2000)` suspends and frees it.
</details>

<details><summary>500 coroutines launched on <code>Dispatchers.Main</code>, each doing a suspending network call.</summary>

All 500 are concurrent, interleaving on one thread at their suspension points. None runs in parallel, and none blocks the others while waiting.
</details>

<details><summary>A callback API wrapped with <code>suspendCoroutine</code>, and the screen closes while waiting.</summary>

The Job can be marked cancelled, but plain `suspendCoroutine` does not complete that suspension merely because its Job was cancelled: the callback still needs to resume it. Cancellation does not automatically unregister the listener; whether it is removed depends on the wrapper/API. Use `suspendCancellableCoroutine` with cancellation cleanup when bridging a cancellable listener.
</details>
